The/gflag matches all occurrences of the pattern, rather than just the first one.

The/iflag makes the match case-insensitive: /abc/i matches "abc", "AbC", "ABC".

The/dflag (the Indices flag) is used to find thestart and end positionsof substrings captured by regular expression groups.

It does not change the regex match. It provides metadata about the result.

The array returned byexec()ormatch()includes an additionalindicesproperty: An array of pairs representing the [start, end] index of a specific capture group.

You can check if a regex has the /d flag using the hasIndices property.

The/sflag allows the . (dot) metacharacter to match newline characters (\n) in addition to any other
character.

The/m(multiline) flag specifies amultiline match.

The/mflag affects the behavior of^and$.

^specifies a match at the start of a string.

$specifies a match at the end of a string.

With/mset,^and$will match the start and end of each line,
in a multiline string, in addition to the start and end of the entire string.

The/yflag performs a "sticky" search from the lastIndex property of the RegExp object.

The/yflag lets a match start at the exact position where the last match ended.

The/uflag enables full Unicode support in a regular expression.

By default, JavaScript RegExp treats 4-byte Unicode characters
(like emojis or less common symbols) as two separate 2-byte "surrogate" code units.

The/uflag treats the pattern as a sequence of Unicode code points,
which is important for correctly handling of characters outside the Basic Multilingual Plane (BMP).

Without the /u flag, regular expressions are not "Unicode-aware".

The/vflag is an upgrade to the /u flag for better Unicode support.

It enables more Unicode-related features.

